<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dzud">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dzud</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dzud is a Mongolian term for a catastrophic winter, when heavy snow, ice or extreme cold prevents livestock from grazing and causes mass deaths of animals. Severe dzud and droughts in the first century BCE killed great numbers of livestock and brought famine to the steppe, contributing to the weakening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A zud, or dzud, is a periodic disaster in the steppe, semi-desert and desert regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, in which large numbers of livestock die, mainly from starvation because particularly severe conditions stop them grazing. In winter it may be caused by an impenetrable ice crust and in summer by drought. Mongolian herders recognise several kinds.</p><p>A tsagaan or white zud follows heavy snowfall that covers the grass. A khar or black zud comes from a lack of snow, which leaves both animals and people without water, and mostly affects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a>. A tumur or iron zud occurs when a short thaw is followed by freezing, so that the snow melts and refreezes into an ice cover. A huiten or cold zud occurs when very low temperatures and strong winds last for several days. A havsarsan zud combines at least two of these.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> a proverb says that the djut has seven relatives, naming seven severe conditions, among them summer drought, early winter, deep snow, winter rains, an ice crust and a blizzard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Traditional protection includes drying and storing cut grass in summer to feed the animals, and stacking blocks of dried sheep and goat dung, called argal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and kizyak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, into walls that shelter the animals from wind and that can also be burnt as winter fuel. Herders move seasonally, wintering in valleys protected by mountains. Under the communist regime the state regulated herd sizes to prevent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a>. After the economy was deregulated in the 1990s and worldwide demand for cashmere rose, the number of goats grew greatly. Goats damage pasture by nibbling at the roots and with their hooves, which leads to desertification, and climate change has brought snowier winters and stronger droughts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A zud can kill over a million head of livestock in a winter. A record loss of almost seven million in 1944 was later surpassed. Three zuds in a row in 1999-2000, 2000-2001 and 2001-2002 killed a combined 11 million animals. In the winter of 2009-2010 snow covered 80 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, extreme cold lasted almost 50 days in Uvs, 9,000 families lost their entire herds and 33,000 more lost half, and by May 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> reported that eight million animals, about 17 percent of the country's livestock, had died. The zud of 2023-2024 killed 7.1 million animals by early June.</p><p>Herders who lose all their animals often move to cities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> is ringed by settlements of wooden houses without roads, water or sewers, where displaced herders may find no work and sink into poverty, while others take dangerous illegal mining jobs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Han armies and their allies continued to contest control of the Tarim Basin, and heavy snows (<b>dzud</b>), droughts and livestock epidemics recorded in the first century BCE killed great numbers of animals and people, weakening the base of Xiongnu power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
